I think you have confused ends with means here.  The desired effect is
a modification of the present and future, specifically one's state of
physical health.  The means is a conscious modification of one's
memory, which need not have any relation to "reality", however you
define it.  (That's not a debate I want to start right now.)

This exercise is no different than the old method for getting rid of
public-speaking jitters, which is to imagine your audience all dressed
in nothing but their underclothes.  Are they really half-naked?
Doesn't matter -- you're calmer and more relaxed.  Shamanism goes much
more deeply into this form of sensualization (visualization involving
all senses) in an effort to gain some measure of control over the body
(= mind).

Your brain is part of your body, and by changing your thinking, you
change the way your body functions.  It is not illogical to suppose
that one could find some specific thought-process that would trigger
or enhance the body's healing mechanisms, as they are already
triggered involuntarily -- or, for that matter, any other of our
bodies' well-known and far-ranging abilities for self-preservation and
advancement.
	For example, most drugs other than antibiotics do not act
directly on pathogens -- they merely trick our bodies into functioning
correctly.  Therefore, shamanism is no different than any other drug,
except it is ingested in the form of sensory input rather than a
chemical, and acts on our nervous system instead of our immune system
and other internal equilibria.

